## Basic Flutter Widget Tree

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

void main() {
  runApp(const MyApp());
}

class MyApp extends StatelessWidget {
  const MyApp({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return const MaterialApp(
      home: HomePage(),
    );
  }
}

class HomePage extends StatelessWidget {
  const HomePage({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(
        title: const Text('Widget Tree'),
      ),
      body: const Center(
        child: Text('Hello Flutter'),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

The conceptual tree is:

```text
MyApp
└── MaterialApp
    └── HomePage
        └── Scaffold
            ├── AppBar
            │   └── Text
            └── Center
                └── Text
```

## Extracting a Meaningful Widget

Instead of keeping everything inside `HomePage`:

```dart
class WelcomeSection extends StatelessWidget {
  const WelcomeSection({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return const Column(
      mainAxisSize: MainAxisSize.min,
      children: [
        Icon(
          Icons.flutter_dash,
          size: 64,
        ),
        SizedBox(height: 16),
        Text('Welcome to Flutter'),
      ],
    );
  }
}
```

Then:

```dart
class HomePage extends StatelessWidget {
  const HomePage({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return const Scaffold(
      body: Center(
        child: WelcomeSection(),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

The page now communicates intent clearly:

```text
HomePage
  ↓
WelcomeSection
```

## Passing Configuration Into Widgets

```dart
class UserCard extends StatelessWidget {
  final String name;
  final String role;

  const UserCard({
    super.key,
    required this.name,
    required this.role,
  });

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Card(
      child: Padding(
        padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16),
        child: Column(
          mainAxisSize: MainAxisSize.min,
          crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.start,
          children: [
            Text(
              name,
              style: const TextStyle(
                fontSize: 20,
                fontWeight: FontWeight.bold,
              ),
            ),
            const SizedBox(height: 4),
            Text(role),
          ],
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```

Usage:

```dart
const UserCard(
  name: 'Arjun',
  role: 'Flutter Developer',
)
```

The widget receives configuration rather than reaching into global state.

## Composition Example

```dart
class ProfilePage extends StatelessWidget {
  const ProfilePage({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return const Scaffold(
      body: SafeArea(
        child: Padding(
          padding: EdgeInsets.all(24),
          child: Column(
            children: [
              UserCard(
                name: 'Arjun',
                role: 'Flutter Developer',
              ),
              SizedBox(height: 24),
              ProfileActions(),
            ],
          ),
        ),
      ),
    );
  }
}

class ProfileActions extends StatelessWidget {
  const ProfileActions({super.key});

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Row(
      mainAxisAlignment: MainAxisAlignment.center,
      children: [
        FilledButton(
          onPressed: () {},
          child: const Text('Follow'),
        ),
        const SizedBox(width: 12),
        OutlinedButton(
          onPressed: () {},
          child: const Text('Message'),
        ),
      ],
    );
  }
}
```

The resulting tree is:

```text
ProfilePage
└── SafeArea
    └── Padding
        └── Column
            ├── UserCard
            │   └── Card
            └── ProfileActions
                └── Row
                    ├── FilledButton
                    └── OutlinedButton
```

## Reusable Widget With Callback

Callbacks will be studied more deeply later, but the basic composition pattern is important now:

```dart
class ActionButton extends StatelessWidget {
  final String label;
  final VoidCallback onPressed;

  const ActionButton({
    super.key,
    required this.label,
    required this.onPressed,
  });

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return FilledButton(
      onPressed: onPressed,
      child: Text(label),
    );
  }
}
```

Usage:

```dart
ActionButton(
  label: 'Save',
  onPressed: () {
    print('Save pressed');
  },
)
```

The widget owns the presentation while the caller owns the action.

## Const Widget Configuration

When a widget and its configuration can be compile-time constants, use `const`:

```dart
const Text('Hello');
```

And:

```dart
const UserCard(
  name: 'Arjun',
  role: 'Flutter Developer',
)
```

A `const` widget can participate in Flutter's widget identity and rebuild optimizations, but `const` should be understood as a language and object-creation feature rather than as a magic solution for all performance problems.

## Practical Exercise

Build the following tree without creating one giant widget:

```text
DashboardPage
├── DashboardHeader
├── UserSummaryCard
├── QuickActions
│   ├── ActionButton
│   ├── ActionButton
│   └── ActionButton
└── RecentActivity
    ├── ActivityItem
    ├── ActivityItem
    └── ActivityItem
```

Requirements:

```text
DashboardPage
→ composition only

DashboardHeader
→ header UI

UserSummaryCard
→ user information

QuickActions
→ action layout

ActionButton
→ reusable action presentation

RecentActivity
→ activity section

ActivityItem
→ individual activity presentation
```

Do not introduce external state management yet.

The goal is to master widget composition.
